            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Fold type (as described)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Product weight</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Folded size (L x W x H)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon T101 Connect</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FeatherLight folding frame</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">180 lb (Horizon)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">46 x 34 x 61 in. (Horizon)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Jogtica</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Handrails detach, frame folds down</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3G Cardio Pro Runner X</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding, compact storage</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">216 lb (listing)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon T202</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hydraulic folding system</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">187 lb (Horizon)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">48 x 34 x 62 in. (Horizon)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F50</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Kick-release with gas-shock assist</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">170.6 lb (SOLE)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">39 x 34 x 62 in. (SOLE)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            A folded treadmill stands about 61 to 62 inches tall, which for a five-foot user means the top
            of the deck is above head height. Check that the locking catch is within reach, and that you
            can release it to lower the deck without stretching. If you will fold it every day, prefer the
            machines with a stated gas-shock or hydraulic assist. If you will rarely fold it, the fold type
            matters less than the step-up and reach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-or-walking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Picks Suit Running, and Which Only Walking?</h2>
<p>
            Two of the five are running treadmills: the Horizon T202 and the 3G Cardio Pro Runner X both
            reach 12 mph. The Horizon T101 Connect and Sole F50 reach 10 mph, which covers walking, jogging
            and most steady running for a shorter runner, whose stride needs less deck. The Jogtica is a
            walking treadmill with a 6 mph top speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Incline matters for shorter walkers too, because an incline lets you raise the effort without
            lengthening your stride. The T101 Connect reaches 10% and the F50 has 12 levels; the Pro Runner X
            has 15 levels. If steep walking is your main workout, our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a> guide ranks machines on incline range first. And if you would rather avoid a monthly fee, all five
            picks run without a paid membership; 3G Cardio says so explicitly, and Jogtica says its app is
            optional. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a> page lists more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes Short People Make Buying a Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Each of these comes from buying a treadmill sized for an average adult without checking the figures that affect a shorter one.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for deck you will not use</h3>
              <p>A 60-inch belt is for running at speed. A shorter walker or jogger gets the same comfort from 55 inches, on a machine that is usually cheaper, lighter and shorter in the room. Buy the deck your stride needs.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming "low deck" means a number</h3>
              <p>Many listings say low deck or easy step-up and never give a figure. Treat a listing without a stated step-up height as unknown, and ask the seller or the maker before you order.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not checking the console reach</h3>
              <p>A console at shoulder height means reaching up and forward to change speed or stop. Prefer handrail controls, dials within a forearm’s reach or a low overall height, and check that you can press stop without leaning.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaving the safety key cord loose</h3>
              <p>On a shorter user the cord is relatively long, so you can drift further back before it pulls. Clip it to your waistband at the front, take up slack and stand forward on the belt.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most short people the best treadmill is the Horizon T101 Connect</strong>:
            a 7 1/8-inch step-on height, a 20 by 55-inch deck that suits a shorter stride, dial controls within
            reach and a lifetime frame and motor warranty, for about $784. If you only walk, the Jogtica senior
            treadmill’s 2.9-inch deck and full-length rails are the easiest to use; if you run, the 3G Cardio Pro
            Runner X pairs a 6.5-inch step with 12 mph; for a 60-inch deck, the Horizon T202; and for the lowest
            console, the 49-inch-tall Sole F50.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, buy on step-up height, reach and fold before deck size, and do not pay for a
            60-inch belt unless you run fast. Where a maker does not publish a figure, as SOLE does not for the
            F50’s step-up, ask before you order. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">belt size guide</a> shows what deck your stride needs, and the <a href="/best-low-profile-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">low profile picks</a> cover the lowest decks and fold-flat machines.
          </p>
