        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill for short people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most, the Horizon T101 Connect: a 7 1/8-inch step-on height published by Horizon, a 20 by 55-inch belt, dial controls and a lifetime frame and motor warranty, for about $784 on Amazon on 24 November 2026. For walking only, the Jogtica senior treadmill has a 2.9-inch deck; for running, the 3G Cardio Pro Runner X has a 6.5-inch step-up.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is a good step-up height for a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For a shorter user, 7 inches or less feels like an ordinary stair, and 4 inches or less like stepping onto a mat. Many full-size home treadmills are 8 inches or more. Few makers publish the figure, so ask the seller if the listing does not state it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do short people need a shorter treadmill belt?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They can use one. Belt length follows stride, and a shorter stride needs less deck: about 48 inches for walking under 5 foot 3, and 55 inches for jogging. Only fast running needs 60 inches, whatever your height.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill too tall for someone who is 5 feet?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some are. The console and handrails on many full-size machines are set for average-height adults. Look for a low overall height (the Sole F50 is 49 inches), controls on the handrails or a dial within reach, and a step-up of 7 inches or less.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a short person fold a treadmill alone?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually, if the fold has a gas-shock or hydraulic assist, as the Sole F50 and Horizon T202 listings describe. Check that you can reach the locking catch when the deck is upright, since a folded treadmill stands about 61 to 62 inches tall.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are walking pads good for short people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For slow walking, yes: the deck is only a few inches off the floor and the belt length suits a shorter stride. They lack handrails, a console and speeds above 4 to 6 mph, so a short person who wants to jog or hold on is better served by a low step-up treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

